## 4. Experiments

### 4.1 E1 — Six-step commutation: {{ r.e01_sixstep.analytic.sine_120_pct_of_peak | fmt(1) }}% is a sine-EMF number

A 120-degree six-step drive applies each phase for 120 electrical degrees and leaves it off for 60 degrees. With an ideal sinusoidal back-EMF and ideal 120-degree current blocks, the instantaneous torque is the sum of two phase products. The ripple is not a switching artifact; it is the shape of the conduction pattern.

The exact result is

\[
\frac{\Delta T}{T_{\rm peak}} = 1-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = 0.1339745962,
\]

or {{ r.e01_sixstep.analytic.sine_120_pct_of_peak | fmt(4) }}%, and

\[
\frac{\Delta T}{T_{\rm mean}} = \frac{\pi(\sqrt{3}-3/2)}{3\sqrt{3}} = 0.1402978691,
\]

or {{ r.e01_sixstep.analytic.sine_120_pct_of_mean | fmt(4) }}%. These are the same physical ripple normalized to two different denominators. The sampled construction gives {{ r.e01_sixstep.cases.sine_emf_120_blocks.pct_of_peak | fmt(4) }}% of peak and {{ r.e01_sixstep.cases.sine_emf_120_blocks.pct_of_mean | fmt(4) }}% of mean, with a sampling residual of {{ r.e01_sixstep.sampling_error_pp | sci(2) }} of peak-to-peak.

The qualifier is the EMF shape. With an ideal trapezoidal EMF and ideal 120-degree blocks, the product is constant and the ripple is {{ r.e01_sixstep.cases.trapezoid_emf_120_blocks.pct_of_peak | fmt(3) }}%. A trapezoidal EMF with sinusoidal current is not constant: the sampled ripple is {{ r.e01_sixstep.cases.trapezoid_emf_sine_current.pct_of_peak | fmt(3) }}%. The corpus’s 13.4% is therefore a statement about a *sinusoidal* machine driven by *block* currents, not about six-step drives in general.

A second hidden condition is alignment. The best case is zero offset, as expected. At 15 electrical degrees of commutation misalignment the ripple rises to {{ r.e01_sixstep.misalignment.at_plus_15_deg.pct_of_peak | fmt(2) }}%; at 30 degrees it is {{ r.e01_sixstep.misalignment.at_plus_30_deg.pct_of_peak | fmt(2) }}%. Hall placement, sensor delay, and phase-order errors all enter through this angle. A drive that measures {{ r.e01_sixstep.analytic.sine_120_pct_of_peak | fmt(1) }}% on a bench with perfect alignment can measure {{ r.e01_sixstep.misalignment.at_plus_15_deg.pct_of_peak | fmt(0) }}% after a harness change without any change in the control law.

![Six-step torque ripple and misalignment sensitivity.]({{ figures[0] }})

**Verdict.** C01 supported; C02 supported only for the ideal trapezoid. The practical lesson is to state the EMF shape and the alignment, not just the percentage.

### 4.2 E2 — SVPWM: {{ r.e02_svpwm.linear_limit.gain_pct | fmt(2) }}% is headroom, not a waveform identity

The linear limit of sinusoidal PWM is \(V_{\rm phase,peak}/V_{\rm dc}=1/2\). Space-vector PWM reaches \(1/\sqrt{3}\). The ratio is

\[
\frac{1/\sqrt{3}}{1/2}-1 = \frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}-1 = 0.1547005384,
\]

or {{ r.e02_svpwm.linear_limit.gain_pct | fmt(4) }}%. The sampled limits are {{ r.e02_svpwm.linear_limit.spwm | fmt(6) }} and {{ r.e02_svpwm.linear_limit.svpwm | fmt(6) }}; the gain is {{ r.e02_svpwm.linear_limit.gain_pct | fmt(6) }}%, matching the analytic value.

The implementation identity is more useful than the percentage. SVPWM is SPWM plus the min-max zero sequence:

\[
u_0 = \tfrac{1}{2}\left(\max(u_a,u_b,u_c)+\min(u_a,u_b,u_c)\right).
\]

The line-line error between the two constructions is {{ r.e02_svpwm.equivalence.svpwm_line_line_error | sci(2) }} V, and the dwell-time construction agrees to {{ r.e02_svpwm.equivalence.dwell_line_line_error | sci(2) }} V. At the test modulation index the maximum zero-sequence magnitude is {{ r.e02_svpwm.equivalence.max_zero_sequence_abs | fmt(3) }}.

The third-harmonic claim is where the compression bites. The exact min-max zero sequence contains a third harmonic of

\[
\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{8\pi} = 0.2067483358,
\]

and the sampled value is {{ r.e02_svpwm.common_mode.minmax_third | fmt(6) }}. The “1/6 third harmonic” injection has a third harmonic of {{ r.e02_svpwm.common_mode.one_sixth_third | fmt(6) }}. Both reach the same linear limit ({{ r.e02_svpwm.common_mode.third_injection_linear_limit | fmt(6) }}), because the line-line fundamental is unchanged; they differ in common-mode voltage and therefore in bearing current, EMI, and leakage. At \(m=1\), the common-mode RMS of the exact min-max sequence is {{ r.e02_svpwm.common_mode.cm_rms_minmax_at_m1 | fmt(4) }}, versus {{ r.e02_svpwm.common_mode.cm_rms_one_sixth_at_m1 | fmt(4) }} for the 1/6 injection, {{ ((1 - r.e02_svpwm.common_mode.cm_rms_one_sixth_at_m1 / r.e02_svpwm.common_mode.cm_rms_minmax_at_m1) * 100) | fmt(0) }}% lower. The identity “SVPWM = 1/6 third harmonic” is a qualified equivalence, not a definition.

![SVPWM linear limit and the exact min-max common-mode spectrum.]({{ figures[1] }})

**Verdict.** C03 supported; C04 supported; C05 qualified.

### 4.3 E3 — Dead time: the formula is right, the average model is not enough

The dead-time experiment uses \(V_{\rm dc}=350\) V, \(f_1=50\) Hz, \(f_{\rm sw}=10\) kHz, \(t_d=800\) ns, \(L=5\) mH, \(R=0.5\) Ω, \(m=0.4\), eight fundamental cycles, and 200 ns steps. The base case gives a fundamental current of {{ r.e03_deadtime.base.i1_a | fmt(2) }} A, a fifth harmonic of {{ r.e03_deadtime.base.i5_a | fmt(4) }} A ({{ r.e03_deadtime.base.i5_pct | fmt(4) }}%), and a seventh harmonic of {{ r.e03_deadtime.base.i7_a | fmt(4) }} A ({{ r.e03_deadtime.base.i7_pct | fmt(4) }}%). The fifth-to-seventh ratio is {{ r.e03_deadtime.base.i5_over_i7 | fmt(3) }}. The sixth harmonic in dq is {{ r.e03_deadtime.base.dq6_pct | fmt(4) }}%.

The fundamental voltage error is {{ r.e03_deadtime.base.v_error_fund_v | fmt(4) }} V, against the analytic

\[
\Delta V = \frac{4}{\pi}\frac{t_d}{T_{\rm sw}}V_{\rm dc} = \frac{4}{\pi}(0.008)(350) = {{ r.e03_deadtime.base.theory_error_fund_v | fmt(4) }}\ \text{V},
\]

\(= {{ r.e03_deadtime.base.theory_error_fund_v | fmt(4) }}\) V. The difference is {{ ((r.e03_deadtime.base.v_error_fund_v - r.e03_deadtime.base.theory_error_fund_v) / r.e03_deadtime.base.theory_error_fund_v * 100) | fmt(2) }}%. The average phase-voltage error is \(t_d f_{\rm sw} V_{\rm dc}={{ r.e03_deadtime.ideal_error_v | fmt(1) }}\) V, which is the DC term that an average model would subtract.

The interesting result is the average model. It removes the fundamental error ({{ r.e03_deadtime.average_model.v_error_fund_v | fmt(3) }} V), but it does not remove the low-order current harmonics: the fifth is still {{ r.e03_deadtime.average_model.i5_pct | fmt(4) }}% and the seventh {{ r.e03_deadtime.average_model.i7_pct | fmt(4) }}%, close to the base case. The reason is that the dead-time error is not a constant DC voltage in the phase legs; it is a square wave at the fundamental frequency whose sign follows the phase current. Averaging it over a switching period produces a fundamental-frequency voltage, but the harmonic content seen by the current controller remains. A controller that compensates only the average error will reduce the fundamental distortion and leave the fifth/seventh current ripple.

The modulation sweep makes the normalization explicit. At \(m=0.1\) the fifth harmonic is {{ r.e03_deadtime.modulation_sweep[0].i5_pct | fmt(3) }}%; at \(m=0.2\) it is {{ r.e03_deadtime.modulation_sweep[1].i5_pct | fmt(3) }}%; at \(m=0.4\) it is {{ r.e03_deadtime.modulation_sweep[2].i5_pct | fmt(3) }}%. The percentage is roughly inversely proportional to \(m\), because the fundamental current scales with \(m\) while the dead-time voltage error does not. A dead-time percentage quoted without a modulation index is not a property of the drive.

![Dead-time current harmonics and fundamental voltage error.]({{ figures[2] }})

**Verdict.** C06 supported; C07 supported. The average-model result is a qualifier for any claim that “dead-time compensation removes the distortion”.

### 4.4 E4 — M/T speed measurement: the crossover is exact, the counts-per-revolution is not

The M method counts encoder pulses in a fixed gate time; the T method measures the period between edges. With \(N\) counts per revolution and a clock \(f_{\rm clk}\), the crossover is

\[
n^* = \frac{60}{N}\sqrt{\frac{f_{\rm clk}}{T_g}}.
\]

For \(N=1000\) lines and \(T_g=10\) ms, the crossover is {{ r.e04_mt_speed.crossover["1MHz"].counts_1000 | fmt(1) }} rpm at 1 MHz, {{ r.e04_mt_speed.crossover["72MHz"].counts_1000 | fmt(1) }} rpm at 72 MHz, and {{ r.e04_mt_speed.crossover["216MHz"].counts_1000 | fmt(1) }} rpm at 216 MHz. The one-count M resolution at 1 MHz is {{ r.e04_mt_speed.analytic.m_resolution_rpm | fmt(1) }} rpm.

Table 2. Speed error at 1 MHz, \(N=1000\) lines, \(T_g=10\) ms.

| Speed (rpm) | M error (%) | T error (%) | M error (rpm) | T error (rpm) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for row in r.e04_mt_speed.tables["1MHz"][6:] -%}
| {{ row.speed_rpm | fmt(0) }} | {{ row.m_err_pct_max | fmt(3) }} | {{ row.t_err_pct_max | fmt(3) }} | {{ row.m_err_max_rpm | fmt(1) }} | {{ row.t_err_max_rpm | fmt(1) }} |
{% endfor %}

The crossover formula predicts the balance near {{ r.e04_mt_speed.crossover["1MHz"].counts_1000 | fmt(0) }} rpm: at {{ r.e04_mt_speed.tables["1MHz"][6].speed_rpm | fmt(0) }} rpm the M error is {{ r.e04_mt_speed.tables["1MHz"][6].m_err_pct_max | fmt(3) }}% and the T error is {{ r.e04_mt_speed.tables["1MHz"][6].t_err_pct_max | fmt(3) }}%. Above it, T degrades as \(n^2\): at {{ r.e04_mt_speed.tables["1MHz"][9].speed_rpm | fmt(0) }} rpm the T error is {{ r.e04_mt_speed.tables["1MHz"][9].t_err_max_rpm | fmt(1) }} rpm ({{ r.e04_mt_speed.tables["1MHz"][9].t_err_pct_max | fmt(2) }}%), while M is {{ r.e04_mt_speed.tables["1MHz"][9].m_err_max_rpm | fmt(1) }} rpm ({{ r.e04_mt_speed.tables["1MHz"][9].m_err_pct_max | fmt(3) }}%).

The hidden condition is \(N\). If “1000 lines” is read as 1000 counts per revolution, the numbers above hold. With a 4× quadrature decoder, \(N=4000\) and every crossover is four times lower ({{ r.e04_mt_speed.crossover["1MHz"].counts_4000 | fmt(1) }} rpm at 1 MHz, {{ r.e04_mt_speed.crossover["72MHz"].counts_4000 | fmt(1) }} rpm at 72 MHz). A factor-of-four error in the decoder convention moves the recommended method by two octaves.

![M and T speed error against speed.]({{ figures[3] }})

**Verdict.** C08 supported; C09 supported, with the counts-per-revolution convention stated.

### 4.5 E5 — K_t and K_e: the {{ r.e05_kt_ke.comparison.kt_dc_vs_foc_pct | fmt(2) }}% is the same 2/√3

The ODrive D5065 270 Kv motor specifies K_v = 270 rpm/V line-line amplitude, seven pole pairs, and K_t = 0.031 Nm/A. The line-line back-EMF constant is

\[
K_e^{LL} = \frac{60}{2\pi K_v} = {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.ke_ll_v_s_per_rad | fmt(10) }}\ \text{V}\cdot\text{s/rad},
\]

and the phase-peak constant is \(K_e^{LL}/\sqrt{3}=0.0204195887\). The flux linkage follows from the phase-peak EMF divided by the electrical angular frequency: \(\psi = K_e^{LL}/(\sqrt{3}p)\), where \(p=7\) is the pole-pair count. The stored value is \(\psi = {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.psi_wb | fmt(7) }}\) Wb, or {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.psi_mwb | fmt(4) }} mWb.

The three K_t conventions are:

| Convention | Rule | K_t (Nm/A) | Ratio to FOC peak |
|---|---|---|---|
| DC / 120° | \(9.5493/K_v\) | {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_dc_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }} | {{ r.e05_kt_ke.comparison.ratio_dc_to_foc | fmt(5) }} |
| FOC peak phase | \(8.2699/K_v\) | {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_foc_peak_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }} | 1 |
| RMS phase | \(11.6955/K_v\) | {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_rms_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }} | {{ r.e05_kt_ke.comparison.ratio_rms_to_foc | fmt(5) }} |

The DC rule is {{ r.e05_kt_ke.comparison.kt_dc_vs_foc_pct | fmt(3) }}% above the FOC peak rule; equivalently, the FOC value is {{ (-r.e05_kt_ke.comparison.kt_foc_vs_dc_pct) | fmt(3) }}% below the DC value. The factor is exactly \(2/\sqrt{3}=1.1547\), the same factor that appears in the SVPWM linear-limit gain. The datasheet’s 0.031 Nm/A is {{ r.e05_kt_ke.comparison.datasheet_vs_foc_pct | fmt(3) }}% above the FOC peak value, which is what one expects if the datasheet uses line-line peak voltage and peak phase current.

At 85 A the FOC convention gives {{ r.e05_kt_ke.at_85a.torque_nm | fmt(4) }} Nm at 1000 rpm, with mechanical power {{ r.e05_kt_ke.at_85a.p_mech_w | fmt(3) }} W and electrical power {{ r.e05_kt_ke.at_85a.p_elec_w | fmt(3) }} W; the power balance residual is {{ r.e05_kt_ke.at_85a.power_balance_error_pct | sci(2) }}%. At 45 A the torque is {{ r.e05_kt_ke.at_85a.torque_45a_nm | fmt(4) }} Nm.

The point is not that 9.5493 is “wrong”. It is that the sentence “K_t = 9.5493/K_v” is missing a subscript. The same motor has K_t = {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_dc_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }}, {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_foc_peak_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }}, or {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_rms_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }} Nm/A depending on whether the current is DC, peak phase, or RMS phase. The datasheet’s footnote is the convention witness; without it, the {{ r.e05_kt_ke.comparison.datasheet_vs_foc_pct | fmt(2) }}% agreement is a coincidence.

![One motor, four K_t numbers.]({{ figures[4] }})

**Verdict.** C10 qualified: the DC rule is correct in its convention, not universal.

### 4.6 E6 — I2C pull-ups: 4.7 kΩ is a Standard-mode component

The I2C timing budget has two constraints. The minimum pull-up is set by the sink current:

\[
R_{p,\min} = \frac{V_{\rm DD}-V_{\rm OL}}{I_{\rm OL}},
\]

and the maximum by the rise time:

\[
R_{p,\max} = \frac{t_r}{0.8473\,C_b},
\]

where \(0.8473=\ln(7/3)\) is the exact coefficient for a 30–70% rise. The mode constants from UM10204 are: Standard 100 kHz, \(t_r=1000\) ns, \(C_b=400\) pF, \(I_{\rm OL}=3\) mA; Fast 400 kHz, \(t_r=300\) ns, \(C_b=400\) pF, \(I_{\rm OL}=3\) mA; Fast-mode Plus 1 MHz, \(t_r=120\) ns, \(C_b=550\) pF, \(I_{\rm OL}=20\) mA. \(V_{\rm OL}=0.4\) V in all three.

A 4.7 kΩ pull-up at 3.3 V supports at most {{ r.e06_i2c.traps.four_k7_max_cb_standard_pf | fmt(1) }} pF in Standard mode, {{ r.e06_i2c.traps.four_k7_max_cb_fast_pf | fmt(1) }} pF in Fast mode, and {{ r.e06_i2c.modes[2].max_cb_at_4k7_pf | fmt(1) }} pF in Fast-mode Plus. The ubiquitous 4.7 kΩ is therefore a Standard-mode part. At 100 pF it is fine in Standard mode, marginal-to-invalid in Fast mode (the maximum capacitance is below 100 pF), and invalid in FM+.

Table 3. I2C pull-up windows at 3.3 V. A window is feasible when \(R_{p,\min}\le R_{p,\max}\).

| Case | \(R_{p,\min}\) (Ω) | \(R_{p,\max}\) (Ω) | Margin (Ω) | Feasible |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Standard, 400 pF | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_standard_400pf_3v3.rp_min_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_standard_400pf_3v3.rp_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_standard_400pf_3v3.margin_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ "yes" if r.e06_i2c.case_standard_400pf_3v3.feasible else "no" }} |
| Fast, 400 pF | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fast_400pf_3v3.rp_min_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fast_400pf_3v3.rp_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fast_400pf_3v3.margin_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ "yes" if r.e06_i2c.case_fast_400pf_3v3.feasible else "no" }} |
| FM+, 550 pF, 20 mA | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_20ma.rp_min_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_20ma.rp_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_20ma.margin_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ "yes" if r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_20ma.feasible else "no" }} |
| FM+, 550 pF, 3 mA | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_3ma.rp_min_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_3ma.rp_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_3ma.margin_ohm | fmt(1) }} | {{ "yes" if r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_3ma.feasible else "no" }} |

The Fast-mode 400 pF case is empty: {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fast_400pf_3v3.rp_min_ohm | fmt(1) }} Ω minimum is above {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fast_400pf_3v3.rp_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} Ω maximum. This is not a matter of choosing a different resistor; the timing and sink-current specifications cannot both be met at 3.3 V. The FM+ case is feasible with the correct 20 mA sink, with a window of {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_20ma.rp_min_ohm | fmt(0) }}–{{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_20ma.rp_max_ohm | fmt(0) }} Ω. Reusing the 3 mA sink from Standard/Fast gives a false impossibility: the minimum becomes {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_3ma.rp_min_ohm | fmt(0) }} Ω and the margin is {{ r.e06_i2c.case_fmplus_550pf_3v3_3ma.margin_ohm | fmt(0) }} Ω. The formula did not change; the current convention did.

![I2C pull-up window at 3.3 V.]({{ figures[5] }})

**Verdict.** C11 refuted; C12 refuted; C13 refuted. The general lesson is that a pull-up value is meaningless without the mode, the bus capacitance, and the sink current.

### 4.7 E7 — RS-485: the 680 Ω bias passes unloaded and fails loaded

A failsafe-biased RS-485 bus must keep \(|V_{AB}|\ge 200\) mV when no driver is active. The bias network is a divider between \(V_{\rm DD}\) and ground, loaded by the termination and by the input resistance of every receiver. A 32-unit-load node is 32 × 12 kΩ = 375 Ω in parallel with the termination.

The maximum bias resistor for 200 mV is:

| \(V_{\rm DD}\) | Termination | Load | \(R_{\rm bias,max}\) (Ω) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 3.3 V | 60 Ω | none | {{ r.e07_rs485.bias_limits[0].r_bias_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} |
| 3.3 V | 60 Ω | 32 UL | {{ r.e07_rs485.bias_limits[1].r_bias_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} |
| 3.3 V | 120 Ω | none | {{ r.e07_rs485.bias_limits[2].r_bias_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} |
| 3.3 V | 120 Ω | 32 UL | {{ r.e07_rs485.bias_limits[3].r_bias_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} |
| 5 V | 60 Ω | none | {{ r.e07_rs485.bias_limits[4].r_bias_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} |
| 5 V | 60 Ω | 32 UL | {{ r.e07_rs485.bias_limits[5].r_bias_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} |
| 5 V | 120 Ω | none | {{ r.e07_rs485.bias_limits[6].r_bias_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} |
| 5 V | 120 Ω | 32 UL | {{ r.e07_rs485.bias_limits[7].r_bias_max_ohm | fmt(1) }} |

The common 680 Ω bias at 5 V is the clearest case. Unloaded, it gives {{ r.e07_rs485.cases["5v_680_no_load"] | fmt(4) }} V = {{ (r.e07_rs485.cases["5v_680_no_load"] * 1000) | fmt(1) }} mV, which passes. With 32 unit loads it gives {{ (r.e07_rs485.cases["5v_680_full_load"] * 1000) | fmt(1) }} mV, which fails. A 560 Ω bias gives {{ (r.e07_rs485.cases["5v_560_full_load"] * 1000) | fmt(1) }} mV and passes. At 3.3 V, 430 Ω gives {{ (r.e07_rs485.cases["3v3_430_full_load"] * 1000) | fmt(1) }} mV (fails) and 390 Ω gives {{ (r.e07_rs485.cases["3v3_390_full_load"] * 1000) | fmt(1) }} mV (passes).

The 680 Ω value is not a random error. It is the value that passes the unloaded calculation, which is the calculation a designer does when the schematic shows two terminators and no receivers. The 32-unit-load condition is the hidden variable. This is the cleanest example in the corpus of a claim that is true on the bench and false in the field.

Termination is a second convention trap. RS-485 is specified around 120 Ω, but Cat5e is about 100 Ω. A 120 Ω termination on 100 Ω cable gives a reflection coefficient of {{ r.e07_rs485.reflections[0].gamma_pct | fmt(2) }}% and a return loss of {{ r.e07_rs485.reflections[0].return_loss_db | fmt(2) }} dB; a 150 Ω termination gives \(\Gamma={{ r.e07_rs485.reflections[2].gamma_pct | fmt(2) }}\)% and {{ r.e07_rs485.reflections[2].return_loss_db | fmt(2) }} dB. The 120 Ω is the driver/receiver nominal, not a cable match. For short drops the mismatch is tolerable; for long buses at high data rates it is not.

The DE timing table is a third hidden condition. An 8E1 frame is 11 bits. At 9600 baud a byte is {{ r.e07_rs485.uart_de_timing[0].byte_8e1_us | fmt(1) }} µs and the worst-case write-to-TC interval is {{ r.e07_rs485.uart_de_timing[0].worst_case_write_to_tc_us | fmt(1) }} µs; at 115200 baud the byte is {{ r.e07_rs485.uart_de_timing[2].byte_8e1_us | fmt(1) }} µs; at 921600 baud it is {{ r.e07_rs485.uart_de_timing[3].byte_8e1_us | fmt(1) }} µs. A fixed 10 µs DE hold is generous at 9600 and marginal at 921600.

![RS-485 failsafe bias with and without unit loads.]({{ figures[6] }})

**Verdict.** C14 refuted for a loaded bus; C15 qualified: 120 Ω is the RS-485 nominal, not a Cat5e match.

### 4.8 E8 — PCB traces: IPC-2221 is not conservative at high current

IPC-2221 gives

\[
I = k\,\Delta T^{0.44} A^{0.725},
\]

with \(k=0.048\) for external layers and \(k=0.024\) for internal layers (1 oz copper). At 10 A, 10 °C rise, 1 oz, the external width is {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ipc2221_ext_mm | fmt(2) }} mm and the internal width is {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ipc2221_int_mm | fmt(2) }} mm. The area ratio is exactly

\[
\left(\frac{0.048}{0.024}\right)^{1/0.725} = 2^{1/0.725} = {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ipc2221_int_over_ext_area | fmt(4) }},
\]

which matches the stored {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ratio_2221_int_over_ext | fmt(4) }}. This is an arithmetic artifact: halving \(k\) is equivalent to halving the current for the same area. The “2.6× penalty” is not an independent measurement of internal-layer thermal performance.

The IPC-2152 empirical fit (Brooks–Adam) is

\[
\Delta T = K\,I^a W^b Th^c,
\]

with widths and thickness in mils. The external fit uses \(K=215.3\), \(a=2\), \(b=-1.15\), \(c=-1.0\); the internal fit uses \(K=200\), \(a=1.9\), \(b=-1.10\), \(c=-1.52\). At the same 10 A/10 °C/1 oz point, the external fit is {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ipc2152_ext_fit_mm | fmt(2) }} mm and the internal fit is {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ipc2152_int_fit_mm | fmt(2) }} mm. The external fit is {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ratio_2152_over_2221_ext | fmt(3) }}× the IPC-2221 width; a 2026 chart reading is {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ipc2152_ext_chart_mm | fmt(1) }} mm, or {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.chart_over_2221_width | fmt(3) }}×.

Table 4. 50 mm trace at 10 A, 10 °C, 1 oz.

