### 4.9 E9 — Unipolar PWM: ripple and loss are different “halves”

The H-bridge experiment uses \(V_{\rm dc}=48\) V, \(f_1=100\) Hz, \(f_{\rm sw}=20\) kHz, \(L=5\) mH, \(R=2\) Ω, \(i_0=5\) A, \(m=0.6\), four fundamental periods, and 2,000 steps per switching period. Four schemes are compared:

- **Bipolar**: both legs switch complementarily; the output is \(+V_{\rm dc}\) or \(-V_{\rm dc}\).
- **Unipolar, single-leg**: one leg switches at \(f_{\rm sw}\), the other holds; the output is \(+V_{\rm dc}\) or 0.
- **Unipolar, dual-leg aligned**: both legs switch at \(f_{\rm sw}\), aligned; the output is \(+V_{\rm dc}\), 0, or \(-V_{\rm dc}\), with the zero state used for the off-time.
- **Unipolar, dual-leg phase-shifted**: both legs switch at \(f_{\rm sw}\), shifted by half a period; the output is a three-level waveform with a negative segment at \(m=0.6\).

Table 5. Ripple and loss at \(m=0.6\).

| Scheme | Ripple p-p (A) | Ratio to bipolar | Negative-voltage fraction | Transitions per \(T_{\rm sw}\) | Loss proxy |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Bipolar | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[0].ripple_pp_a | fmt(4) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[0].ripple_ratio_to_bipolar | fmt(3) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[0].v_neg_fraction | fmt(3) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[0].switching_events_per_switching_period | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[0].loss_proxy_rel | fmt(3) }} |
| Unipolar single-leg | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[1].ripple_pp_a | fmt(4) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[1].ripple_ratio_to_bipolar | fmt(3) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[1].v_neg_fraction | fmt(3) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[1].switching_events_per_switching_period | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[1].loss_proxy_rel | fmt(3) }} |
| Dual-leg aligned | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[2].ripple_pp_a | fmt(4) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[2].ripple_ratio_to_bipolar | fmt(3) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[2].v_neg_fraction | fmt(3) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[2].switching_events_per_switching_period | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[2].loss_proxy_rel | fmt(3) }} |
| Dual-leg shifted | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[3].ripple_pp_a | fmt(4) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[3].ripple_ratio_to_bipolar | fmt(3) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[3].v_neg_fraction | fmt(3) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[3].switching_events_per_switching_period | fmt(1) }} | {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[3].loss_proxy_rel | fmt(3) }} |

The analytic values are close: bipolar {{ r.e09_hbridge.analytic.bipolar_pp_a | fmt(4) }} A, single-leg {{ r.e09_hbridge.analytic.unipolar_single_pp_a | fmt(4) }} A, dual-leg aligned {{ r.e09_hbridge.analytic.unipolar_dual_aligned_pp_a | fmt(4) }} A. The single-leg ratio is {{ r.e09_hbridge.analytic.ratio_single_over_bipolar | fmt(3) }}, which is \(2m/(1+m)\) at \(m=0.6\). The dual-leg aligned ratio is {{ r.e09_hbridge.analytic.ratio_dual_aligned_over_bipolar | fmt(3) }}, which is \(m/(1+m)\).

The claim “unipolar halves ripple” is true only for the dual-leg aligned scheme, and only because it uses the zero state to reduce the volt-second ripple. It is false for the single-leg scheme, where the ratio is {{ r.e09_hbridge.m_sweep[2].single_over_bipolar | fmt(3) }} at \(m=0.6\) and approaches 1 as \(m\to 1\). It is also false for the naive dual-leg phase-shifted scheme, where the phase shift produces a negative-voltage segment: the negative-voltage fraction is {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[3].v_neg_fraction | fmt(3) }} and the ripple ratio is {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[3].ripple_ratio_to_bipolar | fmt(3) }}.

The loss claim is the mirror image. The single-leg scheme has two transitions per switching period instead of four, so its loss proxy is {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[1].loss_proxy_rel | fmt(3) }}. The dual-leg aligned scheme has four transitions and a loss proxy of {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[2].loss_proxy_rel | fmt(3) }} — it halves ripple but not loss. Thus “unipolar halves ripple” and “unipolar halves loss” are both half-truths, and they are true for different schemes.

The modulation sweep makes the asymptote explicit. At \(m=0.2\) the single-leg ratio is {{ r.e09_hbridge.m_sweep[0].single_over_bipolar | fmt(3) }}; at \(m=0.4\) it is {{ r.e09_hbridge.m_sweep[1].single_over_bipolar | fmt(3) }}; at \(m=0.6\) it is {{ r.e09_hbridge.m_sweep[2].single_over_bipolar | fmt(3) }}; at \(m=0.8\) it is {{ r.e09_hbridge.m_sweep[3].single_over_bipolar | fmt(3) }}; at \(m=0.9\) it is {{ r.e09_hbridge.m_sweep[4].single_over_bipolar | fmt(3) }}. The “0.5” is the \(m\to0\) limit of \(2m/(1+m)\), not a general result.

![Unipolar PWM ripple and loss.]({{ figures[8] }})

**Verdict.** C18 qualified; C19 supported only for the single-leg scheme.

