The torque high-load slope is {{ r.e10_paderborn.torque_fit.coefficients.i_q | fmt(4) }} Nm/A and the motoring slope is {{ r.e10_paderborn.torque_fit_motoring.coefficients.i_q | fmt(4) }} Nm/A; the difference is absorbed by the reluctance term and the operating range. The torque temperature coefficient is {{ r.e10_paderborn.torque_fit.alpha_pm_pct_per_k | fmt(4) }}%/K high-load and {{ r.e10_paderborn.torque_fit_motoring.alpha_pm_pct_per_k | fmt(4) }}%/K motoring, while the q-axis back-EMF coefficient is {{ r.e10_paderborn.uq_fit.alpha_pm_pct_per_k | fmt(4) }}%/K. A direct torque measurement of a PMSM should show the magnet temperature coefficient, because \(T\propto \psi i_q\) (plus reluctance); the torque channel does not. It is nearly flat. The dataset description says the torque is derived from the set currents and voltages; the data are consistent with a fixed-parameter torque model. A real dataset can be real and still not validate what it appears to validate.

The voltage-derived torque law is \(1.5[k_f i_q+(k_d-k_q)i_d i_q]\). It predicts the measured torque with a scale of {{ r.e10_paderborn.torque_from_voltage.scale | fmt(6) }}, an offset of {{ r.e10_paderborn.torque_from_voltage.offset_nm | fmt(4) }} Nm, \(R^2={{ r.e10_paderborn.torque_from_voltage.r2 | fmt(6) }}\), and RMSE {{ r.e10_paderborn.torque_from_voltage.rmse_nm | fmt(4) }} Nm. The scale is within {{ ((r.e10_paderborn.torque_from_voltage.scale - 1) * 100) | fmt(2) }}% of unity. The torque law from the voltage channel and the torque channel agree because they are built from the same currents and voltages, not because the torque sensor is independent.

The power balance tests the dq convention. With the 1.5 factor, the median \(P_{\rm elec}/P_{\rm mech}\) is {{ r.e10_paderborn.power_balance.median_p_elec_over_p_mech_1p5 | fmt(4) }}, the mean is {{ r.e10_paderborn.power_balance.mean_p_elec_over_p_mech_1p5 | fmt(4) }}, and the 5th–95th percentile range is {{ r.e10_paderborn.power_balance.p05_1p5 | fmt(4) }}–{{ r.e10_paderborn.power_balance.p95_1p5 | fmt(4) }}. The median efficiency is {{ r.e10_paderborn.power_balance.median_efficiency_1p5 | fmt(4) }}. Without the 1.5 factor, the median is {{ r.e10_paderborn.power_balance.median_p_elec_over_p_mech_1p0 | fmt(4) }}, corresponding to an apparent efficiency of {{ r.e10_paderborn.power_balance.median_apparent_efficiency_1p0 | fmt(4) }} ({{ (r.e10_paderborn.power_balance.median_apparent_efficiency_1p0 * 100) | fmt(0) }}%). The 1.5 factor is not an arbitrary convention; the data reject the alternative.

![Temperature coefficients and the dq power balance.]({{ figures[9] }})

**Verdict.** C20 refuted: the torque channel is not independent. C21 refuted: the 1.5 factor is fixed by the data. C22 supported: the magnet coefficient is {{ r.e10_paderborn.uq_fit.alpha_pm_pct_per_k | fmt(3) }}%/K, inside the NdFeB range.

