## 5. Cross-cutting patterns

The ten experiments are different, but the failures are not. Five patterns account for every qualified or refuted claim in Table 1.

**5.1 Normalization.** The same physical ripple is {{ r.e01_sixstep.analytic.sine_120_pct_of_peak | fmt(1) }}% of peak and {{ r.e01_sixstep.analytic.sine_120_pct_of_mean | fmt(1) }}% of mean (E1). The same K_t is {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_dc_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }}, {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_foc_peak_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }}, or {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_rms_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }} Nm/A depending on the current convention (E5). A dead-time percentage is inversely proportional to the modulation index (E3). The number is not the quantity; the denominator is part of the quantity.

**5.2 Convention.** The 4.7 kΩ pull-up is a Standard-mode component (E6). The 3 mA sink current is a Standard/Fast specification, not an FM+ specification; using it for FM+ manufactures a false impossibility (E6). The IPC-2221 internal-layer coefficient is a halved current, not a measured penalty (E8). The K_t formula is a convention, not a law (E5).

**5.3 Hidden loading.** The 680 Ω RS-485 bias passes unloaded and fails with 32 unit loads (E7). The 4.7 kΩ pull-up passes at 100 pF in Standard mode and fails in Fast mode (E6). The IPC-2221 table assumes 1 oz copper and a 10 °C rise; a different copper thickness or temperature changes the answer (E8). The load is often the difference between a bench demo and a product.

**5.4 Model-derived data.** The Paderborn torque channel is real data, but it behaves like a fixed-parameter model: nearly temperature-flat torque, temperature-sensitive voltage (E10). A dataset can be large, public, and licensed, and still not contain the independent variable its name suggests. Provenance is part of the evidence.

**5.5 Arithmetic artifacts and asymptotes.** The IPC-2221 {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ipc2221_int_over_ext_area | fmt(1) }}× is exactly \(2^{1/0.725}\) (E8). The SVPWM {{ r.e02_svpwm.linear_limit.gain_pct | fmt(2) }}% is exactly \(2/\sqrt{3}-1\), the same factor that separates DC and FOC K_t (E2, E5). The “unipolar halves ripple” claim is the \(m\to0\) limit of \(2m/(1+m)\), not a general result (E9). An identity can be exact and still be misread as a measurement; an asymptote can be exact and still be misread as a general rule.

Table 7. The patterns and their remedies.

| Pattern | Examples | Consequence | Remedy |
|---|---|---|---|
| Normalization | {{ r.e01_sixstep.analytic.sine_120_pct_of_peak | fmt(1) }}% vs {{ r.e01_sixstep.analytic.sine_120_pct_of_mean | fmt(1) }}%; K_t {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_dc_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }} vs {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.kt_foc_peak_nm_per_a | fmt(5) }} | The number is right, the comparison is wrong | State the denominator and the current convention |
| Convention | {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.dc_rule_per_kv | fmt(2) }}/K_v vs {{ r.e05_kt_ke.constants.foc_rule_per_kv | fmt(2) }}/K_v; 3 mA vs 20 mA; IPC k=0.048 vs 0.024 | A formula is applied outside its home | State the mode, the layer, and the unit system |
| Hidden loading | 680 Ω unloaded vs 32 UL; 4.7 kΩ at 100 pF; 1 oz/10 °C | Bench pass, field fail | State the load, the capacitance, and the copper |
| Model-derived data | Paderborn torque | A real dataset validates the wrong thing | Report provenance and test the channel’s temperature dependence |
| Arithmetic artifact | IPC {{ r.e08_ipc.case_10a_10c.ipc2221_int_over_ext_area | fmt(1) }}×; SVPWM {{ r.e02_svpwm.linear_limit.gain_pct | fmt(2) }}%; unipolar {{ r.e09_hbridge.schemes[1].loss_proxy_rel | fmt(1) }} | An identity is mistaken for a measurement | Derive the factor; distinguish identity from asymptote |

The practical checklist is short. Before quoting a number, ask: *normalized to what, in which convention, under what load, from which channel, and is it an identity or an asymptote?* The checklist is more valuable than any single corrected constant in this paper.

